 - [Mesh Jobs APIs](../website/docs/docs/concurrency/index.md#durable-jobs) · [Runtime state and schema](../compiler/mesh-rt/src/jobs/schema.sql) · [Compiled proof](../compiler/meshc/tests/e2e_durable_jobs.rs) · [PostgreSQL test entry point](../scripts/verify-postgres-jobs.sh)
+
+### Experimental trusted persistent workers
+
+On GNU/Linux with Linux 5.11 or later, `Jobs.start_effects` accepts an optional
+`trusted_persistent_workers` array of configured `{"worker":"name","version":1}`
+pairs. Only those exact claimed versions use persistent workers. Omit the
+array to give every attempt a fresh process. Use the default for any handler
+whose code or transitive dependencies have not been audited for reuse.
+
+Trusted handlers run synchronously on the worker's main thread. They may use
+ordinary Mesh values, synchronous operations, and the registered
+`Jobs.effect` calls. They must keep all attempt state in that invocation and
+release any opened resources before returning. They cannot spawn actors,
+start background jobs or callbacks, retain Mesh references, or rely on
+process globals, thread-local state, or native constructors being initialized
+again. The runtime terminates a trusted child that tries to spawn an actor. Later
+claims of that worker version use strict execution on that runner.
+FFI and other process-wide or asynchronous facilities require application
+review. Code that uses unmanaged threads, child processes, signal callbacks,
+persistent handles, or external side effects outside the named registry
+belongs on the strict path. The file descriptor inventory catches ordinary
+descriptor leaks. This opt-in is a trusted application contract, with the
+same OS user and filesystem permissions as the runner.
+
+Each occupied slot uses one child executable and one relay thread. On a clean
+`complete` return their PID and thread can serve another attempt. The child
+receives a fresh private socket and managed process context for each attempt.
+Its environment omits database URLs, and the launch closes inherited
+descriptors above standard I/O. Every call uses the current claim's durable
+token, cancellation and lease fence, the admitted application login over TLS,
+and its own commit receipt after full session reset. The relay drops the
+attempt socket and authority before the runner can settle. Retry, failure,
+invalid replies, timeouts, stops, or a lost authority retire the child before
+another assignment. An unknown application result is never replayed within
+that attempt. Existing durable retry and recovery rules still apply.
+
+A reusable child watchdog enforces the hard deadline and latest confirmed
+lease even if the runner is suspended. Each assignment has a new generation,
+and a renewal or shutdown deadline can affect only that generation. Linux
+sends SIGKILL on parent death. Use the strict path when a workload requires
+fresh initialization of process-wide state for each attempt.
